var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"pluto_tests_git/","page":"Examples","text":"\n<p>The static version of <b>PlanckFunctions.jl</b> Pluto notebook is available at :<a href=\"../planck_test_git.html\">planck_test_git.html</a>\n.</p\n\nNotebook file with PlanckFunctions.jl usage examples are available at Pluto notebooks.","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"PlanckFunctions/#Functions-for-blackbody-thermal-emission-(and-its-derivatives)-calculation","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"","title":"Functions for blackbody thermal emission (and its derivatives) calculation"},{"category":"module","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.PlanckFunctions","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"PlanckFunctions\n\nA comprehensive suite of tools for evaluating properties of the Planck thermal emission spectrum,  including spectral intensity (radiance), integrated band power, and their ratios. \n\nThe module provides exact analytical derivatives with respect to both wavelength (λ)  and temperature (T), optimized for high-performance physics and pyrometry calculations.\n\nCore Design Principles\n\nDerivative Naming Convention:  For any base function foo, its first and second temperature derivatives are available via ∇ₜfoo and ∇²ₜfoo.\nCombined Evaluation:  Functions prefixed with Dₜ (e.g., Dₜfoo) evaluate the base value, the first derivative, and the second derivative simultaneously, returning them as a single Tuple to prevent redundant calculations.\nFunctional Operators:  The symbols ∇ₜ and ∇²ₜ can be used directly as operators on functions, mapping a base function to its corresponding derivative function (e.g., PlanckFunctions.∇ₜ(PlanckFunctions.ibb) -> ∇ₜibb). The symbol ∫ₗ applied on function mapps it to its wavelength integrator , e.g. \n\nPhysical Units\n\nWavelength (λ): Microns (μm)\nTemperature (T): Kelvins (K)\n\n\n\nAPI Reference\n\nSpectral Radiance & Core Functions\n\nibb: Spectral intensity (spectral radiance) in [W/m²⋅sr⋅μm].\n∇ₜibb: First derivative of spectral intensity with respect to temperature [W/m²⋅sr⋅μm·K].\n∇²ₜibb: Second derivative of spectral intensity with respect to temperature [W/m²⋅sr⋅μm·K²].\n\nAll this functions has also and integrated versions , which can be obtained both by direct name  or by applying the ∫ₗ ∫ₗ operator \n\nDₜibb: Base functions, its first and second derivative.\nibb!,  ∇ₜibb!,  ∇²ₜibb!,  Dₜibb! - in-place versions \n∇ₗibb: First derivative of spectral intensity with respect to wavelength [W/m²⋅sr⋅μm²].\n∇²ₗibb: Second derivative of spectral intensity with respect to wavelength [W/m²⋅sr⋅μm³].\n\nIntegrated Band Power\n\nband_power, ∇ₜband_power, ∇²ₜband_power: Total integrated intensity within a specified wavelength region along with its temperature derivatives [W/m²⋅sr].\n\nAnalytical Ratios (Continuous Pyrometry)\n\nspectral_ratio, ∇ₜspectral_ratio, ∇²ₜspectral_ratio: Ratio of spectral intensities at two distinct discrete wavelengths (useful for ratio pyrometers).\nspectral_band_ratio, ∇ₜspectral_band_ratio, ∇²ₜspectral_band_ratio: Ratio of integrated spectral intensities for two distinct wavelength bands.\n\nAveraging & Continuum Mechanics\n\nplanck_averaged: Planck-averaged spectral quantity (e.g., total emittance).\nplanck_averaged_attenuation: Planck-averaging applied specifically to spectral attenuation coefficients.\nrosseland_averaged_attenuation: Rosseland-averaging applied to spectral attenuation coefficients.\nweighted_average: General-purpose function to compute a custom weighted average over a spectrum.\n\nDiscrete/Tabular Integration (Experimental Data Pyrometry)\n\nplanck_weighted, ∇ₜplanck_weighted, ∇²ₜplanck_weighted, Dₜplanck_weighted: Integrates a discrete, wavelength-dependent experimental quantity (such as emissivity or detector response) weighted by the Planck function and its derivatives.\nplanck_weighted_ratio, ∇ₜplanck_weighted_ratio, ∇²ₜplanck_weighted_ratio, Dₜplanck_weighted_ratio: Computes the ratio of two independent Planck-weighted discrete quantities and its analytical temperature derivatives.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.PlanckFunctions"},{"category":"constant","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.C₁","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"C₁ constant for Planck function multiplier in [W⋅μm⁴/(m²⋅sr)]\nsource https://physics.nist.gov/\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.C₁"},{"category":"constant","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.C₂","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"C₂ constant for Planck spectral intensity exponent in [μm⋅K]\nsource https://physics.nist.gov/\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.C₂"},{"category":"constant","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.C₃","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"C₃ constant of Wien's displacement law [μm⋅K]\nsource https://physics.nist.gov/\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.C₃"},{"category":"constant","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.C₃_∇₁","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Constant of the first derivaitve Wien's displacement \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.C₃_∇₁"},{"category":"constant","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.C₃_∇₂","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Constant of the second derivaitve Wien's displacement \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.C₃_∇₂"},{"category":"constant","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.C₄","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"C₄ constant in equation for maximum blackbody intensity [W/(m²⋅μm⋅sr*K⁵)]\nsource https://physics.nist.gov/\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.C₄"},{"category":"constant","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.c_big","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Speed of light in vacuum\n`c` [https://physics.nist.gov/] , m s^-1\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.c_big"},{"category":"constant","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.h_big","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Planck const\n`h` [https://physics.nist.gov/] , J Hz^-1\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.h_big"},{"category":"constant","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.kB_big","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Boltzmann const \n`kb` [https://physics.nist.gov/] , J K^-1\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.kB_big"},{"category":"constant","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.σ","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Stefan-Boltzmann constant [W/(m²*K⁴)]\n\nsource https://physics.nist.gov/ \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.σ"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#ChainRulesCore.frule-Tuple{Any, typeof(band_power), Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"frule((Δself, ΔT), ::typeof(band_power), T; λₗ=0.0, λᵣ=Inf, tol=1e-8)\n\nForward-mode AD rule for band_power. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ChainRulesCore.frule"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#ChainRulesCore.frule-Tuple{Any, typeof(spectral_ratio), Number, Number, Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"frule((Δself, ΔT), ::typeof(band_power), T; λₗ=0.0, λᵣ=Inf, tol=1e-8)\n\nForward-mode AD rule for band_power. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ChainRulesCore.frule"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#ChainRulesCore.frule-Union{Tuple{TT}, Tuple{Tλ}, Tuple{Any, typeof(ibb), Tλ, TT}} where {Tλ<:Number, TT<:Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"frule((Δself, Δλ, ΔT), ::typeof(ibb), λ::Number, T::Number)\n\nCustom forward-mode automatic differentiation rule for the Planck function ibb(λ, T)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ChainRulesCore.frule"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#ChainRulesCore.rrule-Union{Tuple{TT}, Tuple{Tλ}, Tuple{typeof(ibb), Tλ, TT}} where {Tλ<:Number, TT<:Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"rrule(::typeof(ibb), λ::Number, T::Number)\n\nCustom reverse-mode automatic differentiation rule (pullback) for ibb(λ, T).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ChainRulesCore.rrule"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#ChainRulesCore.rrule-Union{Tuple{TT}, Tuple{typeof(band_power), TT}} where TT<:Number","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"ChainRulesCore.rrule(\n                    ::typeof(band_power), \n                    T::TT; λₗ::Number , λᵣ::Number\n                ) where { TT<:Number}\n\nCustom reverse-mode automatic differentiation rule (pullback) for band_power(T; λₗ = λₗ , λᵣ = λᵣ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ChainRulesCore.rrule"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.Dₗibb-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Dₗibb(λ,T)\n\nReturns a three-element tuple of (1.bb intensity,2.its first and 3.second derivative  with respect to the wavelentgh)\n\nArguments:\n\nλ - wavelength, μm T - temperature, K\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.Dₗibb"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.Dₜband_power-Tuple{Any, Val{true}}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Dₜband_power(T , skip_second_derivative::Val{true} ; λₗ=0.0 , λᵣ=Inf , tol=1e-8)\n\nreturns the value and its derivative\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.Dₜband_power"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.Dₜband_power-Tuple{Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Dₜband_power(T ; λₗ=0.0 , λᵣ=Inf , tol=1e-8)\n\nreturns all derivatives \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.Dₜband_power"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.Dₜibb!-Tuple{Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}, AbstractVector, Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"    Dₜibb!(input_tuple, λ::AbstractVector,T)\n\nIn-place filling the tuple of (bb intensity, its first ,and second ) derivatives with  respect to temperature\n\nArguments:\n\ninput_tuple, {Nx0 vector or nothing , Nx0 vector or nothing, Nx0 vector or nothing} λ - wavelength, μm, [Nx0] T - temperature, K    Out: input_tuple filled with ibb , dIbb/dT , d²Ibb/dT²\n\nThis version is slightly faster than calling each derivative separately\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.Dₜibb!"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.Dₜibb-Tuple{Number, Number, Val{true}}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Dₜibb( λ::Number, T::Number , skip_second_derivative::Val{true})\n\nSkips the second derivative evaluation Dₜibb( λ, T , Val(true))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.Dₜibb"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.Dₜibb-Tuple{Number, Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Dₜibb( λ::Number, T::Number)\n\nreturns a tuple of (value, first derivative, second derivative)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.Dₜibb"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.Dₜibb-Union{Tuple{Q}, Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector{Q}}} where Q","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Dₜibb(λ::AbstractVector,T::AbstractVector)\n\nCalculates tuple of (Ibb,dIbb/dT,d²Ibb/dT²) calculated according to:\n\nd²Ibb/dT² = C₁*(eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)²)*(C₂/(λ⁶*T³))*[(C₂/(λ*T))*(2*eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)-1)-2], d²Ibb/dT² = C₁*(eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)²)*(a₁/(λ⁵*T²))*[a₁*(2*eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1) -1)-2], a₁=C₂/(λ*T), a₂ = 1/(eᵃ¹-1) ,  a₃ = eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1) , d²Ibb/dT² = C₁*a₂*a₃*(a₁/(λ⁵*T²))*[a₁*(2*a₃ - 1))-2]     as far as          Ibb = (λ⁻⁵)* C₁*a₂     and          dIbb/dT = C₁*a₃*a₂*a₁*(1/(λ⁵*T)) = a₃*a₁*Ibb/T      hense         d²Ibb/dT² = C₁*a₂*a₃*a₁*(1/(λ⁵*T²))*[a₁*(2*a₃ - 1))-2]              = [a₃*a₁*Ibb/T^2]*[a₁*(2*a₃ - 1))-2]                  = [(dIbb/dT)/T]*[a₁*(2*a₃ - 1))-2] \n\nArguments:\n\nλ - wavelength region, μm T - temperature, Kelvins\n\nReturns:\n\n(Ibb , dIbb/dT , d²Ibb/dT²)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.Dₜibb"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.Dₜplanck_weighted-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{L}, Tuple{Q}, Tuple{AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, D}} where {Q<:Number, L<:Number, D<:Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Dₜplanck_weighted(e::AbstractVector{Q}, λ::AbstractVector{L}, T::D) where {Q <: Number, L <: Number, D <: Number}\n\nEvaluates the integral of e(λ) over three weighting functions of  Planck's function ,  its first- and second- derivaitve:  ibb, ∇ₜibb и ∇²ₜibb\n\nReturns six-elements tuple (integrals and normalizers) : ( ∫α(λ)ibb(λ,T)dλ , ∫α(λ)∇ₜibb(λ,T)dλ , ∫α(λ)∇²ₜibb(λ,T)dλ)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.Dₜplanck_weighted"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.Dₜplanck_weighted_ratio-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{Q}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, D}} where {D<:Number, Q<:Number, L<:Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Dₜplanck_weighted_ratio(\n                    α1::AbstractVector{Q}, \n                    λ1::AbstractVector{L},\n                    α2::AbstractVector{Q}, \n                    λ2::AbstractVector{L},\n                    T::D ) where { D <: Number , Q <: Number , L <: Number}\n\nReturns the tuple of the value, first and second derivatives of two weighted values ratio :\n\nArguments\n\nα1 - first wavelength dependent quantity,  λ1 - first wavelengths range, μm α2-  second wavelength dependent quantity,  λ2-  second wavelengths range, μm T - temperature , K\n\nReturns\n\n``( ∫α1⋅g(λ,T)dλ1 / ∫f(α2)g(λ,T)dλ2 , \n\nd/dT [∫α1⋅g(λ,T)dλ1/∫α2⋅g(λ,T)dλ2] , \n\nd²/dT² [ ∫α1⋅g(λ,T)dλ1/∫α2⋅g(λ,T)dλ2 ] )``\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.Dₜplanck_weighted_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.Dₜplanck_weighted_values-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{L}, Tuple{Q}, Tuple{AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, D}} where {Q<:Number, L<:Number, D<:Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Dₜplanck_weighted_values(e::AbstractVector{Q}, λ::AbstractVector{L}, T::D) where {Q <: Number, L <: Number, D <: Number}\n\nEvaluates the integral of e(λ) over three weighting functions of  Planck's function ,  its first- and second- derivaitve:  ibb, ∇ₜibb и ∇²ₜibb\n\nReturns six-elements tuple (integrals and normalizers) : ( ∫α(λ)ibb(λ,T)dλ , ∫α(λ)∇ₜibb(λ,T)dλ , ∫α(λ)∇²ₜibb(λ,T)dλ ,     ∫ibb(λ,T)dλ , ∫∇ₜibb(λ,T)dλ , ∫∇²ₜibb(λ,T)dλ)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.Dₜplanck_weighted_values"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.Dₜpower-Tuple{Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Dₜpower(T)\n\nTuple of total intensity , fisrt and second derivative with respect to temeperature\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.Dₜpower"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.Dₜspectral_band_ratio-Union{Tuple{TL}, Tuple{Tuple{TL, TL}, Tuple{TL, TL}, Number, Val{true}}} where TL<:Number","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Dₜspectral_band_ratio(λ1::NTuple{2, TL}, λ2::NTuple{2,TL}, T::Number , skip_second_derivative::Val{true};  tol = 1e-6) where TL <: Number\n\nIgnores the second derivative evaluation \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.Dₜspectral_band_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.Dₜspectral_band_ratio-Union{Tuple{TL}, Tuple{Tuple{TL, TL}, Tuple{TL, TL}, Number}} where TL<:Number","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Dₜspectral_band_ratio(λ1::NTuple{2, TL}, λ2::NTuple{2,TL}, T::Number;  tol = 1e-6) where TL <: Number\n\nAll spectral band ratio derivatives are in one tuple\n\nArguments\n\nλ1- tuple of left and right wavelength of the first band, in μm. λ2- tuple of left and right wavelength of the second band, in μm. T- Absolute temperature, in K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.Dₜspectral_band_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.Dₜspectral_ratio-Tuple{Number, Number, Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"Dₜspectral_ratio(λ1::Number, λ2::Number, T::Number)\n\nAll spectral ratio derivatives in one tuple\n\nArguments\n\nλ1- First wavelength (usually the shorter one), in μm. λ2- Second wavelength (usually the longer one), in μm. T- Absolute temperature, in K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.Dₜspectral_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.attenuated_band_power-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"    attenuated_band_power(T, τ::AbstractVector, λ::AbstractVector;  tol=1e-6)\n\nEvaluates the integrated band power of BB with temperature T reaching the detector,  accounting for the spectral transmittance profile τ(λ).\n\nArguments:\n\nT - temeperature , K  τ - transmittance λ - wavelength , μm\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.attenuated_band_power"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.a₁₂₃!-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{D}, AbstractVector, Number}} where D","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"a₁₂₃!(amat::AbstractMatrix,λ::AbstractVector,T::Number)\n\nIn-place filling of the intermediate matrix a₁=C₂/(λ*T)  - amat first column a₂ = 1/(eᵃ¹-1)  - amat second column  a₃ = eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1) - amat third column\n\nArguments:\n\namat - matrix of intermediate coefficients size [Nx3], λ - wavelength , μm,  [Nx0], T - temperature,  K,\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.a₁₂₃!"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.a₁₂₃-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{L, F}} where {F, L}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"a₁₂₃(λ::Float64,T::Float64)\n\nArguments:\n\namat - matrix of intermediate coefficients size [Nx3] λ - wavelength , μm,  [Nx0] T - temperature,  K\n\nReturns tuple:\n\n(a1 = C₂/(λ*T), a2 = 1/expm1(a1), a3 = 1 + a2)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.a₁₂₃"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.band_power-Tuple{Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"    band_power(T;λₗ=0.0 , λᵣ=Inf , tol=1e-8)\n\nTotal bb with temperature T integral intensity (∫ibb(λ , T)dλ) within (in-band radiance), [W/(m²⋅sr)] the spectral range λₗ...λᵣ (by default the range is 0...inf) tol - tolerance of intehration\n\nArguments:\n\nT - temperature,Kelvins (optional) λₗ - left wavelength boundary, μm λᵣ - right wavelength boundary, μm tol - intergation tolerance\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.band_power"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.bb_max-Tuple{Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"bb_max(T::Number)\n\nBlackbody instensity at maximum value \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.bb_max"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.bb_max-Tuple{typeof(ibb), Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"bb_max(bb_function , T)\n\nReturns the value of function at a maximum  bb_function can be ibb ,   ∇ₜibb ,  ∇²ₜibb\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.bb_max"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.bright_temperature-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"bright_temperature(i, λ; ϵ=1.0)\n\nEvaluates bright temperature for single wavelength pyrometer  i - measured intensity , λ - wavelength, μm  ϵ - emissivity \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.bright_temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.ibb!-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, AbstractMatrix}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"    ibb!(i::AbstractVector , λ::AbstractVector , amat::AbstractMatrix)\n\nIn-place blackbody intensity with intermediate coefficients provided externally, [W/m2-sr-mkm] Ibb =  C₁*(λ⁻⁵)*a₂ , where a₁=C₂/(λ*T)  - amat first column a₂ = 1/(eᵃ¹-1)  - amat second column \n\nArguments:\n\ni - BB intensity, [Nx0] λ - wavelength in μm,  [Nx0] amat - matrix of intermediate coefficients,  [Nx3]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.ibb!"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.ibb!-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"    ibb!(i::AbstractVector , λ::AbstractVector , T::Number)\n\nIn-place blackbody intensity,  [W/m²⋅sr⋅μm] Ibb = (λ⁻⁵)* C₁/(eᵃ¹-1) , where a₁=C₂/(λT)\n\nArguments:\n\ni - bb intensity vector, [Nx0] λ - wavelength in μm, [Nx0] T - temperature in Kelvins\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.ibb!"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.ibb-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractMatrix}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"ibb(λ::AbstractVector,amat::AbstractMatrix)\n\nBlackbody spectral intensity (spectral radiance)  with intermediate matrix provided externally, [W/m²⋅sr⋅μm] Ibb =  C₁*(λ⁻⁵)*a₂ ,  a₁=C₂/(λ*T)  - amat first column,  a₂ = 1/(eᵃ¹-1)  - amat second column \n\nArguments:\n\namat - matrix of intermediate coefficients,  [Nx3] λ - wavelength in μm,  [Nx0]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.ibb"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.ibb-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"    ibb(λ::AbstractVector,T::AbstractVector)\n\nBlackbody spectral intensity (spectral radiance),  [W/m²⋅sr⋅μm] Ibb = (λ⁻⁵)* C₁/(eᵃ¹-1) , where a₁=C₂/(λ*T)\n\nArguments:\n\nλ - wavelength in μm, [Nx0] T - temperature in Kelvins [Mx0]\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.ibb"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.ibb-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"ibb(λ,T)\n\nBlackbody spectral intensity (spectral radiance), [W/m²⋅sr⋅μm] Ibb = (λ⁻⁵)* C₁/(eᵃ¹-1) , a₁=C₂/(λ*T)\n\nArguments:\n\nλ - wavelength in μm T - temperature in Kelvins\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.ibb"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.planck_averaged-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"planck_averaged(x::AbstractVector, λ::AbstractVector,T::Number)\n\nEvaluates the Planck-averaged value of x(λ) for temperature T:\n\nxᵣ = ∫x(λ)ibb(λ,T)dλ/∫ibb(λ,T)dλ\n\nE.g. can be used to evaluate the integral  from  spectral emissivity\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.planck_averaged"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.planck_averaged_attenuation-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"planck_averaged_attenuation(α::AbstractVector, λ::AbstractVector,T::Number)\n\nPlanck-averaged spectral attenuation coefficient (the summation of spectral scattering and absorption coefficients) α(λ) for temperature T:\n\nαᵣ = (∫(1/α(λ))⋅ibb(λ,T)dλ/∫ibb(λ,T)dλ)⁻¹   \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.planck_averaged_attenuation"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.planck_weighted-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{Q}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, D}} where {D<:Number, Q<:Number, L<:Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"planck_weighted(α::AbstractVector{Q}, \n                    λ::AbstractVector{L},\n                    T::D) where { D <: Number , Q <: Number , L <: Number}\n\nEvaluates ∫α(λ)⋅ibb(λ,T)dλ using numeric integration\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.planck_weighted"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.planck_weighted_ratio-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{Q}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, D}} where {D<:Number, Q<:Number, L<:Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"planck_weighted_ratio(\n                    α1::AbstractVector{Q}, \n                    λ1::AbstractVector{L},\n                    α2::AbstractVector{Q}, \n                    λ2::AbstractVector{L},\n                    T::D) where { D <: Number , Q <: Number , L <: Number}\n\nReturns the ratio of two weighted values  (∫α1(λ)ibb(λ,T)dλ1 / ∫α2(λ)ibb(λ,T)dλ2)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.planck_weighted_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.power-Tuple{Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"power(T)\n\nIntegral (over the wavelength) intensity of BB (radiance)  at temperature T\n\nUnits: W/(m²⋅sr)\n\nArguments:\n\nT - temperature, K\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.power"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.rosseland_averaged_attenuation-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"rosseland_averaged_attenuation(α::AbstractVector, λ::AbstractVector,T::Number)\n\nEvaluates the Rosseland-averaged spectral attenuation coefficient (the summation of spectral scattering and absorption coefficients) α(λ) for temperature T:\n\nαᵣ = (∫(1/α(λ))⋅∇ₜibb(λ,T)dλ/∫∇ₜibb(λ,T)dλ)⁻¹  \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.rosseland_averaged_attenuation"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.second_order_polynomial_fit-NTuple{6, Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"second_order_polynomial_fit(x1,x2,x3,g1,g2,g3)\n\nHardcoded second order polynomial lsqr fitting\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.second_order_polynomial_fit"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.spectral_band_ratio-Union{Tuple{TL}, Tuple{Tuple{TL, TL}, Tuple{TL, TL}, Number}} where TL<:Number","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"spectral_band_ratio(λ1::NTuple{2, TL}, λ2::NTuple{2,TL}, T::Number;   tol = 1e-6) where TL <: Number\n\nThe same as spectral_ratio, but now the band can be wide (not a single wavelength). This function may be useful for two-color pyrometers in case when their working regions  width cannot be ignored \n\nArguments\n\nλ1- tuple of left and right wavelength of the first band, in μm. λ2- tuple of left and right wavelength of the second band, in μm. T- Absolute temperature, in K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.spectral_band_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.spectral_ratio-Tuple{Number, Number, Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"spectral_ratio(λ1::Number, λ2::Number, T::Number)\n\nCalculate the theoretical intensity ratio R =  ( Ibb1/Ibb2) between two wavelengths λ1 and λ2 at temperature T\n\nArguments\n\nλ1- First wavelength (usually the shorter one), in μm. λ2- Second wavelength (usually the longer one), in μm. T- Absolute temperature, in K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.spectral_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.temperature-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"    temperature(i)\n\nThe bb temperature for intensity i corresponding to  wavelength λ \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.tₘ-Tuple{Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"tₘ(λ)\n\nThe temperature of BB having maximum at wavelength λ in Kelvins\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.tₘ"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.units-Tuple{Function}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"units(f::Function)\n\nreturns units string of output quantity  return \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.units"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.weighted-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{Q}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, D, Union{typeof(ibb), typeof(∇²ₜibb), typeof(∇ₜibb)}}} where {D<:Number, Q<:Number, L<:Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"weighted(α::AbstractVector{Q}, \n                    λ::AbstractVector{L},\n                    T::D, \n                    g::Dfunctions) where { D <: Number , Q <: Number , L <: Number}\n\n∫α⋅g(λ,T)dλ where g ∈ {ibb , ∇ₜibb , ∇²ₜibb}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.weighted"},{"category":"function","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.weighted_average","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"weighted_average(α::AbstractVector, \n                    λ::AbstractVector,\n                    T::Number, \n                    g::Dfunctions,\n                    f::F = identity) where F\n\nGeneric function to evaluate the averaged value of some f(x) function of variable x dependent on wavelength λ for temperature T.  Uses linear approximation for the discrete variable and square polynomial  for the g function \n\nxᵣ = ∫f(x)g(λ,T)dλ/∫g(λ,T)dλ the default value of f is identity, e.g. if f = inv: xᵣ = ∫g(λ,T)/x(λ)dλ/∫g(λ,T)dλ\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.weighted_average"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.weighted_value-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{Q}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, D, Union{typeof(ibb), typeof(∇²ₜibb), typeof(∇ₜibb)}}, Tuple{AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, D, Union{typeof(ibb), typeof(∇²ₜibb), typeof(∇ₜibb)}, F}} where {F, D<:Number, Q<:Number, L<:Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"weighted_value(     α::AbstractVector, \n                    λ::AbstractVector,\n                    T , \n                    g::Union{typeof(ibb),typeof(∇ₜibb),typeof(∇²ₜibb)},\n                    f::F = identity) where F <: Function\n\nReturns the tuple of  (∫f(α)g(λ,T)dλ , ∫g(λ,T)dλ)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.weighted_value"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.weighted_values_ratio-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{Q}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, D, Union{typeof(ibb), typeof(∇²ₜibb), typeof(∇ₜibb)}}, Tuple{AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, D, Union{typeof(ibb), typeof(∇²ₜibb), typeof(∇ₜibb)}, F}} where {F, D<:Number, Q<:Number, L<:Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"weighted_values_ratio(\n                    α1::AbstractVector{Q}, \n                    λ1::AbstractVector{L},\n                    α2::AbstractVector{Q}, \n                    λ2::AbstractVector{L},\n                    T::D, \n                    g::Dfunctions,\n                    f::F = identity) where {F   , D <: Number , Q <: Number , L <: Number}\n\nReturns the ratio of two weighted values  (∫f(α1)g(λ,T)dλ1 / ∫f(α2)g(λ,T)dλ2)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.weighted_values_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.λₘ-Tuple{Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"λₘ(T::Number)\n\nThe wavelength (in μm) of bb intensity maximum vs temperature T  argmax(Planck(T))  - Wien's displacement law\n\nArguments:\n\nT - temperature in Kelvins\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.λₘ"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.λₘ-Tuple{Union{typeof(ibb), typeof(∇²ₜibb), typeof(∇ₜibb)}, Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"λₘ(::Union{typeof(ibb) , typeof(∇ₜibb) , typeof(∇²ₜibb)} , T::Number)\n\nThe wavelength (in μm) of bb intensity  , its first or second derivaitve maximum vs temperature T \n\nArguments:\n\nT - temperature in Kelvins\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.λₘ"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇²ₗibb-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇²ₗibb(λ,T)\n\nBB intensity second derivative with respect to the wavelength\n\nArguments:\n\nλ - wavelength, μm T - temperature, K\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇²ₗibb"},{"category":"function","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜ","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇²ₜ(f) \n\nDifferentiation operator returns the derivatives of function\n\nibb , band_power , power , spectral_ratio , spectral_band_ratio\n\nExamples\n\n ∇²ₜ(ibb)          # -> ∇²ₜibb\n ∇²ₜ(band_power)   # ->  ∇²ₜband_power\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜ"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜband_power-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇²ₜband_power(T , band_power_value; λₗ=0.0 , λᵣ=Inf , tol=1e-8)\n\nBand power second derivative with respect to temeprature with band_power  value evaluated externally \n\nArguments:\n\nT - temperature,Kelvins\n\n(optional) λₗ - left wavelength boundary, μm λᵣ - right wavelength boundary, μm tol - intergation tolerance\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜband_power"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜband_power-Tuple{Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇²ₜband_power(T ; λₗ=0.0 , λᵣ=Inf , tol=1e-8)\n\nBand power second derivative with respect to temeprature \n\nArguments:\n\nT - temperature,Kelvins\n\n(optional) λₗ - left wavelength boundary, μm λᵣ - right wavelength boundary, μm tol - intergation tolerance\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜband_power"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜibb!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇²ₜibb!(h::AbstractMatrix{Float64} , λ::AbstractVector{Float64}, T::AbstractVector{Float64})\n\nIn-place bb intensity second order derivative with respect to temperature\n\nd²Ibb/dT² = C₁*(eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)²)*(C₂/(λ⁶*T³))*[(C₂/(λ*T))*(2*eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)-1)-2],\n\nd²Ibb/dT² = C₁*(eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)²)*(a₁/(λ⁵*T²))*[a₁*(2*eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1) -1)-2], a₁=C₂/(λ*T), a₂ = 1/(eᵃ¹-1)  , a₃ = eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1) ,\n\nd²Ibb/dT² = C₁*a₂*a₃*(a₁/(λ⁵*T²))*[a₁*(2*a₃ - 1))-2]\n\nArguments :\n\nh  - to be filled, [Nx0] λ - wavelength in μm, [Nx0] T- tmperature in Kelvins   \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜibb!"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜibb!-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Number, AbstractMatrix}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇²ₜibb!(h::AbstractVector{Float64}, λ::AbstractVector{Float64} , T::Float64 ,amat::AbstractMatrix{Float64})::Nothing\n\nIn-place bb intensity second order derivative with respect to temperature with  intermediate matrix provided externally\n\nd²Ibb/dT² = C₁*(eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)²)*(C₂/(λ⁶*T³))*[(C₂/(λ*T))*(2*eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)-1)-2],\n\nd²Ibb/dT² = C₁*(eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)²)*(a₁/(λ⁵*T²))*[a₁*(2*eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1) -1)-2], a₁=C₂/(λ*T), a₂ = 1/(eᵃ¹-1)   , a₃ = eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1) ,\n\nd²Ibb/dT² = C₁*a₂*a₃*(a₁/(λ⁵*T²))*[a₁*(2*a₃ - 1))-2]\n\nArguments :\n\nh  - to be filled, [Nx0] λ- wavelength in μm, [Nx0] T - temperature in Kelvins amat - matrix of intermediate coefficients,  [Nx3]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜibb!"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜibb!-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇²ₜibb!(h::AbstractVector , λ::AbstractVector , T::Number)\n\nIn-place bb intensity second order derivative with respect to temperature\n\nArguments :\n\nh  - to be filled, [Nx0] λ - wavelength in μm, [Nx0] T - tmperature in Kelvins  \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜibb!"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜibb!-Tuple{AbstractVector, Number, AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇²ₜibb!(h::AbstractVector{Float64},T::Float64,amat::AbstractMatrix{Float64},∇i::AbstractVector{Float64})::Nothing\n\nIn-place bb intensity second order derivative with respect to temperature  with provided both the intermediate matrix amat and the the Planck function first derivative\n\nd²Ibb/dT² = C₁*(eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)²)*(C₂/(λ⁶*T³))*[(C₂/(λ*T))*(2*eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)-1)-2]\n\nd²Ibb/dT² = C₁*(eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)²)*(a₁/(λ⁵*T²))*[a₁*(2*eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1) -1)-2] a₁=C₂/(λ*T) a₂ = 1/(eᵃ¹-1)   , a₃ = eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1) , d²Ibb/dT² = C₁*a₂*a₃*(a₁/(λ⁵*T²))*[a₁*(2*a₃ - 1))-2] as far as      Ibb = (λ⁻⁵)* C₁*a₂ and      dIbb/dT = C₁*a₃*a₂*a₁*(1/(λ⁵*T)) = a₃*a₁*Ibb/T  hense\n\nd²Ibb/dT² = C₁*a₂*a₃*a₁*(1/(λ⁵*T²))*[a₁*(2*a₃ - 1))-2]          = [a₃*a₁*Ibb/T^2]*[a₁*(2*a₃ - 1))-2]              = [(dIbb/dT)/T]*[a₁*(2*a₃ - 1))-2]\n\nArguments :\n\nh  - to be filled, [Nx0] λ - wavelength in μm, [Nx0] amat - matrix of intermediate coefficients,  [Nx3] ∇i - vector of bb intensity first derivatives, [Nx0]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜibb!"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜibb-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇²ₜibb(λ,T)\n\nBB intensity second derivative with respect to temperature\n\nd²Ibb/dT² = C₁*(eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)²)*(C₂/(λ⁶*T³))*[(C₂/(λ*T))*(2*eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)-1)-2] ,\n\nd²Ibb/dT² = C₁*(eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)²)*(a₁/(λ⁵*T²))*[a₁*(2*eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1) -1)-2], a₁=C₂/(λ*T), a₂ = 1/(eᵃ¹-1),   a₃ = eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1) ,\n\nd²Ibb/dT² = C₁*a₂*a₃*(a₁/(λ⁵*T²))*[a₁*(2*a₃ - 1))-2]\n\nArguments :\n\nλ - wavelength in μm T - tmperature in Kelvins         \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜibb"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜplanck_weighted-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{Q}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, D}} where {D<:Number, Q<:Number, L<:Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇²ₜplanck_weighted(α::AbstractVector{Q}, \n                    λ::AbstractVector{L},\n                    T::D) where { D <: Number , Q <: Number , L <: Number}\n\nEvaluates ∫α(λ)⋅∇²ₜibb(λ,T)dλ using numeric integration\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜplanck_weighted"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜplanck_weighted_ratio-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{Q}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, D}} where {D<:Number, Q<:Number, L<:Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇²ₜplanck_weighted_ratio(\n                    α1::AbstractVector{Q}, \n                    λ1::AbstractVector{L},\n                    α2::AbstractVector{Q}, \n                    λ2::AbstractVector{L},\n                    T::D) where { D <: Number , Q <: Number , L <: Number}\n\nReturns the ratio of two weighted values  d²/dT² [ (∫α1(λ)ibb(λ,T)dλ1 / ∫α2(λ)ibb(λ,T)dλ2) ]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜplanck_weighted_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜpower-Tuple{Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇²ₜpower(T)\n\nTotal  intensity second  derivative of BB (radiance)  at temperature T\n\nUnits: W/(m²⋅sr⋅K)\n\nArguments:\n\nT - temperature, K\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜpower"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜspectral_band_ratio-Union{Tuple{TL}, Tuple{Tuple{TL, TL}, Tuple{TL, TL}, Number}} where TL<:Number","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇²ₜspectral_band_ratio(λ1::NTuple{2, TL}, λ2::NTuple{2,TL}, T::Number;  tol = 1e-6) where TL <: Number\n\nSpectral band ratio second derivative\n\nArguments\n\nλ1- tuple of left and right wavelength of the first band, in μm. λ2- tuple of left and right wavelength of the second band, in μm. T- Absolute temperature, in K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜspectral_band_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜspectral_ratio-Tuple{Number, Number, Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇²ₜspectral_ratio(λ1::Number, λ2::Number, T::Number)\n\nSpectral ratio second derivative\n\nArguments\n\nλ1- First wavelength (usually the shorter one), in μm. λ2- Second wavelength (usually the longer one), in μm. T- Absolute temperature, in K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜspectral_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜ∫ibbₗ-Tuple{Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇²ₜ∫ibbₗ(T; λₗ=0.0, λᵣ=Inf)\n\nRelative (with respect to the integral power in the whole spectrum) integral intensity derivative (analytic) of bb intensity fraction  in the spectral range λₗ...λᵣ (by default the range is 0...inf)\n\nArguments:\n\nT - temperature,Kelvins (optional) λₗ - left wavelength boundary, μm λᵣ - right wavelength boundary, μm\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇²ₜ∫ibbₗ"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇ₗibb-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇ₗibb(λ,T)\n\nBB intensity first derivative with respect to the wavelength\n\nArguments:\n\nλ - wavelength, μm T - temperature, K\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇ₗibb"},{"category":"function","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇ₜ","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇ₜ(f) \n\nDifferentiation operator returns the derivatives function for the input function\n\nibb , band_power , power , spectral_ratio , spectral_band_ratio ,  planck_weighted,planck_weighted_ratio and their derivatives e.g ∇ₜband_power etc.\n\nExamples\n\n∇ₜ(ibb)        # -> ∇ₜibb\nF = (∇ₜ∘∇ₜ)(spectral_band_ratio)   #  ->  ∇²ₜspectral_band_ratio\nF(1253.5) # returns the second derivative with respect to temperature of the ratio of two spectral band at temperature 1253.5 K\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇ₜ"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇ₜband_power-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇ₜband_power(T , band_power_value; λₗ=0.0 , λᵣ=Inf)\n\nEvaluates bandpower derivative with respect to temperature with `bandpowervalue` provided externally (this may be usefull if one already has calculated the bandpower itself)\n\nArguments:\n\nT - temperature,Kelvins band_power_value - band_power calculated elswhere\n\n(optional) λₗ - left wavelength boundary, μm λᵣ - right wavelength boundary, μm tol - intergation tolerance\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇ₜband_power"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇ₜband_power-Tuple{Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇ₜband_power(T;λₗ=0.0,λᵣ=Inf,tol=1e-6)\n\nTotal bb with temperature T integral intensity derivative  within (in-band radiance), [W/(m²⋅sr⋅K)] the spectral range λₗ...λᵣ (by default the range is 0...inf) tol - tolerance of integration\n\nArguments:\n\nT - temperature,Kelvins (optional) λₗ - left wavelength boundary, μm λᵣ - right wavelength boundary, μm tol - intergation tolerance\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇ₜband_power"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇ₜibb!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇ₜibb!(g::AbstractMatrix , λ::AbstractVector , T::AbstractVector)\n\nIn-place BB intensity first derivative with respect to temperature a₁=C₂/(λ*T) a₂ = 1/(eᵃ¹-1)    a₃ = eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)  dIbb/dT = C₁*a₃*a₂*a₁*(1/(λ⁵*T))\n\nArguments:\n\ng - vector to be filled, [Nx0] λ - wavelength in μm, [Nx0] T - temperature in Kelvins     \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇ₜibb!"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇ₜibb!-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Any, AbstractMatrix}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇ₜibb!(g::AbstractVector , λ::AbstractVector , T , amat::AbstractMatrix)\n\nIn-place bb intensity first derivative with respect to temperature with externally provided amat  - matrix with columns a₁,a₂,a₃\n\ndIbb/dT = C₁*(eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)²)*(C₂/(λ⁶*T²)), a₁=C₂/(λ*T) , a₂ = 1/(eᵃ¹-1)   , a₃ = eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1) , dIbb/dT = C₁*a₃*a₂*a₁*(1/(λ⁵*T)),\n\nArguments:\n\ng - to be filled, [Nx0] λ - wavelength in μm, [Nx0] T - temperature in Kelvins amat - matrix of intermediate coefficients, [Nx3]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇ₜibb!"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇ₜibb!-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any, AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇ₜibb!(g::AbstractVector , T , amat::AbstractMatrix , bb_intensity::AbstractVector)\n\nIn-place bb intensity first derivative with respect to temperature with externally provided both amat  - matrix with columns a₁,a₂,a₃ and bb_intensity\n\ndIbb/dT = C₁(eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)²)(C₂/(λ⁶T²)) a₁=C₂/(λT) a₂ = 1/(eᵃ¹-1)   #  1/expm1(a1) a₃ = eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1) #  exp(a)/expm1(a) dIbb/dT = C₁a₃a₂a₁(1/(λ⁵T)) as far as Ibb = C₁a₂/λ⁵ dIbb/dT = a₃a₁C₁(a₂/λ⁵)(1/T)=a₃a₁Ibb/T\n\nArguments:\n\ng - to be filled, [Nx0] λ - wavelength in μm, [Nx0] T - temperature in Kelvins amat - matrix of intermediate coefficients, [Nx3]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇ₜibb!"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇ₜibb-Tuple{AbstractVector, Number, AbstractMatrix}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇ₜibb(λ::AbstractVector,T,amat::AbstractMatrix)\n\nBB intensity first derivative with respect to temperature with externally provided matrix of intermediate coefficients dIbb/dT = C₁*(eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)²)*(C₂/(λ⁶*T²)) a₁=C₂/(λ*T) a₂ = 1/(eᵃ¹-1)    a₃ = eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)  dIbb/dT = C₁*a₃*a₂*a₁*(1/(λ⁵*T))\n\nArguments:\n\nλ - wavelength in μm, [Nx0] T - temperature in Kelvins amat - matrix of intermediate coefficients, [Nx3]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇ₜibb"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇ₜibb-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"        ∇ₜibb(λ,T)\n\nBB intensity first derivative with respect to temperature dIbb/dT = C₁(eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)²)(C₂/(λ⁶T²)) `a₁=C₂/(λT)a₂ = 1/(eᵃ¹-1)#  1/expm1(a1)a₃ = eᵃ¹/(eᵃ¹-1)` #  exp(a)/expm1(a) dIbb/dT = C₁a₃a₂a₁(1/(λ⁵*T))\n\nArguments:\n\nλ - wavelength, μm T - temperature, K\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇ₜibb"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇ₜplanck_weighted-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{Q}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, D}} where {D<:Number, Q<:Number, L<:Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"planck_weighted(α::AbstractVector{Q}, \n                    λ::AbstractVector{L},\n                    T::D) where { D <: Number , Q <: Number , L <: Number}\n\nEvaluates ∫α(λ)⋅∇ₜibb(λ,T)dλ using numeric integration\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇ₜplanck_weighted"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇ₜplanck_weighted_ratio-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{Q}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, AbstractVector{Q}, AbstractVector{L}, D}} where {D<:Number, Q<:Number, L<:Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇ₜplanck_weighted_ratio(\n                    α1::AbstractVector{Q}, \n                    λ1::AbstractVector{L},\n                    α2::AbstractVector{Q}, \n                    λ2::AbstractVector{L},\n                    T::D) where { D <: Number , Q <: Number , L <: Number}\n\nReturns the ratio of two weighted values  d/dT [ (∫α1(λ)ibb(λ,T)dλ1 / ∫α2(λ)ibb(λ,T)dλ2) ]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇ₜplanck_weighted_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇ₜpower-Tuple{Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇ₜpower(T)\n\nTotal intensity first derivative of BB (radiance)  at temperature T\n\nUnits: W/(m²⋅sr⋅K)\n\nArguments:\n\nT - temperature, K\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇ₜpower"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇ₜspectral_band_ratio-Union{Tuple{TL}, Tuple{Tuple{TL, TL}, Tuple{TL, TL}, Number}} where TL<:Number","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇ₜspectral_band_ratio(λ1::NTuple{2, TL}, λ2::NTuple{2,TL}, T::Number;   tol = 1e-6) where TL <: Number\n\nFirst derivative of two wide spectral band ratio\n\nArguments\n\nλ1- tuple of left and right wavelength of the first band, in μm. λ2- tuple of left and right wavelength of the second band, in μm. T- Absolute temperature, in K\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇ₜspectral_band_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇ₜspectral_ratio-Tuple{Number, Number, Number}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∇ₜspectral_ratio(λ1::Number, λ2::Number, T::Number)\n\nCalculate the theoretical intensity ratio derivative dR/T =d/dT (  Ibb1/Ibb2 ) between two wavelengths λ1 and λ2 at temperature T\n\nArguments\n\nλ1- First wavelength (usually the shorter one), in μm. λ2- Second wavelength (usually the longer one), in μm. T- Absolute temperature, in K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇ₜspectral_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∇ₜ∫ibbₗ-Tuple{Any}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"    ∇ₜ∫ibbₗ(T; λₗ=0.0, λᵣ=Inf)\n\nRelative (with respect to the integral power in the whole spectrum) integral intensity derivative (analytic) of bb intensity fraction  in the spectral range λₗ...λᵣ (by default the range is 0...inf)\n\nArguments:\n\nT - temperature,Kelvins (optional) λₗ - left wavelength boundary, μm λᵣ - right wavelength boundary, μm\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∇ₜ∫ibbₗ"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∫ibbₗ-Tuple{Q} where Q","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∫ibbₗ(T;λₗ=0.0,λᵣ=Inf,tol=1e-6)\n\nRelative (with respect to the integral power in the whole spectrum) integral intensity of bb in the spectral range λₗ...λᵣ  (by default the range is 0...inf)\n\nArguments:\n\n`T - temperature,Kelvins\n\n(optional)\n\nλₗ - left wavelength boundary, μm λᵣ - right wavelength boundary, μm tol - intergation tolerance\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∫ibbₗ"},{"category":"function","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∫ₗ","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∫ₗ(g)\n∫ₗ(g, λ₁, λ₂)\n∫ₗ(g, λ::AbstractVector, α::AbstractVector)\n\nThe spectral integration operator for Planck radiation quantities.\n\nDepending on the provided argument signature, this operator returns different  highly optimized, type-stable callable objects (functors) designed to minimize  runtime allocations.\n\nArguments\n\ng::Dfunctions: The base Planck function layer (ibb, ∇ₜibb, ∇²ₜibb, or Dₜibb).\nλ₁, λ₂::Number: Spectral band boundaries in microns (μm).\nλ::AbstractVector, α::AbstractVector: Discrete experimental data arrays (wavelength grid and spectral coefficients).\n\n\n\nFull Spectrum Integration (Unary)\n\nWhen applied directly to a single argument, it returns a functor representing the analytical total integrated power over the entire spectrum ([0, ∞)) governed by the Stefan-Boltzmann law.\n\nf = ∫ₗ ibb\nf(1273.5) # Returns σ * T^4\n\n\n\nSpectral Band Integration\n\nWhen given a function and two numeric boundaries, it returns a functor that evaluates the integrated band power within the specific interval [λ₁, λ₂].\n\nf = ∫ₗ(ibb, 2.3, 4.5)\nf(1273.5) # Returns integrated power between 2.3 and 4.5 μm\n\n\n\nTabular Data Integration\n\nWhen given a function and two matching discrete arrays, it returns a functor designed for experimental data pyrometry (e.g., integrating a wavelength-dependent emissivity vector α against the Planck distribution).\n\nf = ∫ₗ(ibb, λ_grid, e_data)\nf(1273.5) # Returns the discrete Planck-weighted integral \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∫ₗ"},{"category":"method","location":"PlanckFunctions/#PlanckFunctions.∫ₗ-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Number, Number}} where F<:Union{typeof(ibb), typeof(∇²ₜibb), typeof(∇ₜibb)}","page":"PlanckFunctions","text":"∫ₗ(f::F , λ₁::Number, λ₂::Number) where F <: Union{typeof(ibb), typeof(∇²ₜibb), typeof(∇ₜibb)}\n\nReturns a callable object which returns the f function integral as a function of temperature\n\nf = ∫ₗ(ibb , 2.3 , 4.5)\nf(1273.5) # returns the value of Planck function integrated over 2.3 - 4.5 spectral range at temperature 1273,5 K\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.∫ₗ"},{"category":"section","location":"TestingData/#Functions-for-blackbody-thermal-emission-(and-its-derivatives)-calculation","page":"TestingData","text":"","title":"Functions for blackbody thermal emission (and its derivatives) calculation"},{"category":"module","location":"TestingData/#TestingData","page":"TestingData","text":"This module contains some  hardcoded data obtained from Berger, Chris G. 'Blackbody Calculator', Optics: The Website, https://www.opticsthewebsite.com/OpticsCalculators . Accessed on 5/14/2025 \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TestingData"},{"category":"constant","location":"TestingData/#Main.TestingData.benchmark_data","page":"TestingData","text":"The data for testing was obtained from\n\nBerger, Chris G. 'Blackbody Calculator', Optics: The Website, https://www.opticsthewebsite.com/OpticsCalculators . Accessed on 5/14/2025\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Main.TestingData.benchmark_data"},{"category":"method","location":"TestingData/#Main.TestingData.read_temperature_data-Tuple{Any}","page":"TestingData","text":"read_temperature_data(tag)\n\nFunctions to read benchmark data spectrum from the default testing data folder\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Main.TestingData.read_temperature_data"},{"category":"method","location":"TestingData/#Main.TestingData.temperatures-Tuple{}","page":"TestingData","text":"temperatures()\n\nReturns numerical array of all temperatures in benchmark\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Main.TestingData.temperatures"},{"category":"method","location":"TestingData/#Main.TestingData.testing_data-Tuple{String}","page":"TestingData","text":"testing_data(tag::String)\n\nreturns benchmark data for specified tag\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Main.TestingData.testing_data"},{"category":"section","location":"","page":"Home","text":"PlanckFunctions.jl is a high-performance, type-stable Julia package designed for computing blackbody thermal emission spectra, integrated band power, and their exact analytical derivatives. \n\nEngineered specifically for optical physics, radiative heat transfer, and radiation pyrometry, the package bypasses numerical differentiation pitfalls by leveraging exact mathematical derivatives combined with robust numerical integration schemes.\n\n","title":"Home"},{"category":"section","location":"#Key-Features","page":"Home","text":"Exact Analytical Derivatives: Fast computation of first and second derivatives with respect to temperature (T) and wavelength (lambda).\nCombined Evaluation (Dₜ Functions): Evaluate a base function and its first two temperature derivatives simultaneously in a single pass, cutting execution time for root-finding algorithms (e.g., Halley's method).\nFunctional Differentiating Operators: Use standard notation (∇ₜ, ∇²ₜ, ∇ₗ, ∇²ₗ) to map a base radiation function directly to its respective analytical derivative function.\nDomain-Specific Language (∫ₗ Operator): Express total, band, and discrete experimental data integrals using standard mathematical syntax.\nAveraged Spectral Coefficients: Built-in support for Rosseland- and Planck-averaged attenuation coefficients and total emittance.\n\n","title":"Key Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Physical-Units","page":"Home","text":"To ensure consistency across physical domains, the package strictly adheres to the following unit convention:\n\nWavelength (lambda): Microns (mutextm)\nTemperature (T): Kelvins (textK)\nSpectral Intensity (I_lambda): Watts per square meter per steradian per micron (textW(textm^2cdottextsrcdotmutextm))\nTotal / Band Power (P): Watts per square meter per steradian (textW(textm^2cdottextsr))\n\n","title":"Physical Units"},{"category":"section","location":"#Core-Usage-and-Examples","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Core Usage & Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"#1.-Basic-Evaluation-and-Exact-Derivatives","page":"Home","text":"Evaluate the Planck blackbody distribution (ibb) and its respective temperature gradients:\n\nusing PlanckFunctions\n\nλ = 1.5   # microns\nT = 1500.0 # Kelvins\n\n# Individual evaluations\nintensity = ibb(λ, T)\ndI_dT     = ∇ₜibb(λ, T)\nd2I_dT2   = ∇²ₜibb(λ, T)\n\n# Combined high-performance evaluation (returns a NTuple{3, Float64})\nval, dval, d2val = Dₜibb(λ, T)","title":"1. Basic Evaluation & Exact Derivatives"},{"category":"section","location":"#2.-Functional-Differentiating-Operators-(ₜ,-ₜ,-ₗ,-ₗ)","page":"Home","text":"Instead of manually typing specific derivative function names, you can use high-level differentiating operators directly as functional mappings. This capability extends natively to temperature derivatives across the entire package ecosystem, as well as wavelength derivatives for core spectral functions.\n\nusing PlanckFunctions\n\n# Temperature derivatives mapped dynamically across the core API\n# (Supports: ibb, power, band_power, spectral_ratio, spectral_band_ratio, etc.)\n(∇²ₜ(ibb) == ∇²ₜibb)     # true\n(∇ₜ(∇ₜibb) == ∇²ₜibb)    # true\n\n# Practical usage on a band power function\ndPower_dT = ∇ₜ(band_power)\ndPower_dT(1500.0; λₗ = 1.2, λᵣ = 4.0)","title":"2. Functional Differentiating Operators (∇ₜ, ∇²ₜ, ∇ₗ, ∇²ₗ)"},{"category":"section","location":"#3.-High-Level-Integration-via-the-ₗ-Operator","page":"Home","text":"The package provides a mathematical syntax sugar operator ∫ₗ that constructs highly optimized, zero-allocation callable functors based on your integration domain.\n\nusing PlanckFunctions\n\n# Unary Operator: Analytical full-spectrum integration (Stefan-Boltzmann law: σ * T^4)\ntotal_power = ∫ₗ(ibb)\ntotal_power(1500.0)\n\n# Band Integration: Continuous integration over a specific spectral band [λ₁, λ₂]\nband_integrator = ∫ₗ(ibb, 1.2, 4.0)\nband_integrator(1500.0) # Returns integrated power within 1.2 - 4.0 μm\n\n# Tabular Integration: Integrates discrete experimental data (e.g., spectral emissivity α) \n# against the Planck distribution over a wavelength grid λ\nλ_grid = collect(range(0.5, 3.0, length=100))\nα_data = rand(100) # Experimental spectral data\n\ndiscrete_integrator = ∫ₗ(ibb, λ_grid, α_data)\ndiscrete_integrator(1500.0)\n\n","title":"3. High-Level Integration via the ∫ₗ Operator"},{"category":"section","location":"#Infrastructure-Capabilities","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Infrastructure Capabilities"},{"category":"section","location":"#1.-Automatic-Differentiation-(AD)-Compatibility","page":"Home","text":"PlanckFunctions.jl defines custom frule and rrule expressions via ChainRulesCore.jl. This allows you to seamlessly plug the package into any modern Julia AD framework (e.g., Zygote) without encountering numerical instabilities or performance degradation during gradient calculation.\n\nusing Zygote, PlanckFunctions\n\n# Compute gradient of spectral intensity\nZygote.gradient(ibb, 2.0, 1500.0)\n\n# AD of band power exactly matches the analytical temperature derivative function\nZygote.gradient(t -> band_power(t; λₗ = 1.2, λᵣ = 4.0), 1500.0)","title":"1. Automatic Differentiation (AD) Compatibility"},{"category":"section","location":"#2.-Physical-Uncertainty-Propagation","page":"Home","text":"With built-in support for Measurements.jl, you can feed values with experimental uncertainties directly into the functions. The package will propagate the errors mathematically using linear error propagation:\n\nusing Measurements, PlanckFunctions\n\n# Automatically returns an intensity value with precise error bounds\nI_measured = ibb(1.5, 1500.0 ± 2.5) \n\n# Propagate error through a multi-band spectral ratio calculation\nratio = spectral_band_ratio((1.0, 2.0), (2.0, 3.0), 1374.5 ± 0.5)","title":"2. Physical Uncertainty Propagation"},{"category":"section","location":"#3.-Symbolic-Representation-and-Code-Generation","page":"Home","text":"When Symbolics.jl is loaded, PlanckFunctions.jl automatically activates a package extension enabling you to convert complex radiation equations into pure symbolic expressions for analytical manipulation or specialized code generation:\n\nusing Symbolics, PlanckFunctions\n\nibb_sym = symbolize(ibb) # Returns the symbolic representation of the Planck function\n\n# Easily differentiate or simplify symbolic equations\ndI_sym = Symbolics.derivative(ibb_sym, T)","title":"3. Symbolic Representation & Code Generation"}]
}
